ed last week  dozens of other canadians though are  still behind bars in china and their  loved ones don't want them forgotten as  abigail beeman explains their  circumstances are different and whether  they have a path home is deeply  uncertain  this new baby is one of six  grandchildren wang bing zhang has never  met the pro-democracy activist has been  a prisoner in china for close to 20  years it's devastating to all of us that  you know we don't know if he'll ever  meet any of them daughter tiana wang  knows he's still alive but that's about  it she says her family was relieved to  see michael cover and michael spavor  back on canadian soil she hopes others  won't be forgotten i don't know if you  know people like my father will ever  receive that level of support and  attention so that you know that's  something that  is  i guess concerning to me that that's  perhaps what it took to  eventually get the release but  but you know we have to be hopeful  wang's father is not a canadian citizen  but deeply connected he and others like  him are likely not counted in official  global affairs canada counts about 115  canadians still imprisoned in china  including four sentenced to death most  recently robert schellenberg whose  family still declines interviews but  released new photos and a statement  still hoping there would be a change in  bob's status i am very angry uyghur  activist and canadian hussein jalil has  been imprisoned since 2006 with no  consular access his wife can't even say  for sure he's alive through those  goldman sachs they can't deal with china  the next step is to bring my husband  back home to canada she ramped up her  calls to government in the wake of the  spavor kovarik homecoming and says no  one called her back there is always more  to do the prime minister was asked about  jaleel's case this week he didn't name  him in his answer we will continue to  advocate  for people who are wrongly imprisoned  around the world there needs to be more  attention paid to these other prisoners  who have received no attention  whatsoever i think the chinese were  surprised at the degree to which we were  able to mount  broader international pressure to uh  raise the michael issue that's not going  to happen  with these other cases  whatever their merits may or may not be  a tough juxtaposition for dozens of  canadian families holding out hope  abigail beeman global news ottawa  next the first national day for truth  and reconciliation what it means and how  it will be marked  we're on the eve of the first national  day for truth and reconciliation and the  hope is it will mean more than just a  day off or a day to wear an orange shirt  the truth and reconciliation commission  called for a day to ensure that public  commemoration of the history and legacy  of residential schools remains a vital  component of the reconciliation process  and to honor the survivors this is  recent history survivors are still here  eric sorenson looks at how the day will  be marked  it is a day of commemoration a long time  in coming considering the enduring  legacy of canada's relationship with  indigenous peoples in halifax a walk in  solidarity for reconciliation took place  st mary's university staff and students  organizing time to reflect and to  acknowledge that on the in tandem we're  walking with people who have been  victimized by systematic racism in  canada the national day for truth and  reconciliation will honor survivors and  the children who died in residential  schools the discovery of hundreds of  unmarked graves at different sites has  horrified canadians many are only  beginning to fully appreciate the pain  and the resilience of indigenous people  i would love canadians to participate  wear an orange shirt reach out to their  local indigenous communities find out  what events are happening attend them  it's a very real thing and  all of those children have relatives  that are alive today that are grieving  school d